eac3to-go
=========

Native media demuxing and audio processing in Go, with eac3to 4.00 behavior
as the compatibility reference. The project is under development. It is not
yet a complete replacement for every Windows eac3to operation.

Linux and Windows amd64 builds are checked in under bin/:

  bin/eac3to
  bin/windows-amd64/eac3to.exe

Keep the matching libexec directory beside each executable for audio
conversions that use the decoder helper. Packages, checksums and matching
decoder source are available at https://github.com/720pixel/eac3to-go/releases.

  make build
  make decoder
  make windows
  make check

Use bin/eac3to --help for available options. Linux builds use native Cgo SSRC
and Aften processing. Windows builds use an isolated checksum-pinned MinGW
toolchain; see scripts/build-windows.py. Media probing and some codecs use
separate FFmpeg/FFprobe executables. Existing system installations and libFDK
support are never replaced by the build scripts.
Disc probing requires FFprobe with libbluray enabled. Keep the FFmpeg/FFprobe
pair together; explicit --ffmpeg and --ffprobe paths take priority.

Dependency discovery searches explicit paths and environment overrides, a pair
beside the executable or under tools/, PATH, then the isolated user installation.
Windows uses .exe filenames and the native Windows PATH. Missing diagnostics name
both required programs and explain setup. --doctor (also -test) checks the actual
programs, codecs and FFprobe's Blu-ray protocol support. The optional matched
decoder is also discovered beside the executable and on PATH.

--download-tools is an explicit setup command. It downloads a matched FFmpeg and
FFprobe pair for Linux/Windows amd64 from BtbN's release branch, verifies the
publisher's SHA-256 digest, and checks both executables and libbluray support
before publication. Existing tools are left alone. The default destination is
an eac3to-go/tools directory inside the user's cache; --tools-dir=DIR selects a
new destination. The archive and failed stages are removed. No administrator
rights, system package installation or PATH changes are used. Separate FFmpeg
builds have their own codec configuration; the matched audio decoder remains
necessary for operations that require that profile.

  eac3to /disc/BDMV
  eac3to /disc/BDMV --main
  eac3to /disc/BDMV -demux -destpath=/output
  eac3to /disc/BDMV --playlist=00001 2: video.h264 3: audio.dts
  eac3to --doctor
  eac3to --download-tools
  eac3to --download-tools --tools-dir=/path/to/new/tools
  eac3to input.wav output.flac
  eac3to input.wav output.wav -25.000 -changeTo24.000 +100ms

Quote paths containing spaces and title selectors such as '1)' when using a
shell. Existing outputs require --force. Unsupported operations fail explicitly.
--allow-unverified enables comparison paths; it does not establish parity.

TrueHD and its embedded Dolby companion are extracted as separate elementary
files. A .thd output contains only checked TrueHD access units. Request -core
on that source for a separate .ac3 file, or .eac3 when that is the actual
companion codec; a mismatched extension is rejected. No compatibility audio
is encoded by -core, and no companion is invented for pure TrueHD.

  eac3to /disc/BDMV --playlist=00001 3: main.thd -keepDialnorm 3: compatible.ac3 -core -keepDialnorm

Native playlist copying applies the checked multi-segment TrueHD postprocess
and retains the separate companion. Timing scans inspect known media PIDs so
unrelated clock and program-table padding cannot block extraction. Transport
boundaries, selected streams and codec frames remain checked. Strict TrueHD
playlist-start trimming and unvalidated physical joins retain their explicit
quality gates.

--playlist-video-window limits video PES to the authored playlist in/out points
while leaving audio framing and delay rules unchanged. It omits video padding
beyond a clip's authored presentation without cutting into a TrueHD major unit.

Interactive terminals open a wizard when launched without a source or with
only a source. It lists playlists with their recommendation evidence, accepts
track numbers or ranges, and asks for an output format and destination for
each selected track. Review the concrete extraction/conversion plan before
entering yes to start. q, EOF or no cancels before processing starts.

  eac3to
  eac3to /disc/BDMV
  eac3to /disc/BDMV --non-interactive

--non-interactive keeps source-only commands in listing mode for scripts.
Existing commands with explicit outputs or -demux remain unattended.
--interactive requests a review even for a fully specified output command.
JSON, report and dry-run operations do not start an automatic wizard.

--report=/path/to/extraction.json writes a successful extraction manifest with
the final output paths and residual mux delays. It is intended for muxers that
need sample/frame-aligned extraction without dropping the remaining offset.
The report is published only after every requested output succeeds.

Playlist selection combines authored main-feature labels, navigation evidence,
clip metadata, picture-language branches and playlist families. Ambiguous discs
require --playlist. It does not claim universal Java/menu emulation.

Track reports also expose validated authored stream metadata. Blu-print project
labels are matched to the complete MPLS timeline, stream numbers, languages,
PIDs and primary angle before they can mark commentary, audio description,
SDH or forced text. JSON includes the original label and its source alongside
FFprobe-compatible dispositions. MPLS stream languages take precedence over
transport language tags. Unknown labels do not establish a role, and channel
counts or short-playlist names never establish commentary or feature alignment.

Keep compressed sources in their original codec for stream extraction. Audio
conversion has codec-specific requirements; lossless output alone does not
make every decoder equivalent. The separate pinned decoder profile has a
bounded Windows comparison corpus. --reference-tail reproduces historical
resampler tail behavior; normal draining preserves the complete filter tail.

Tests use generated format fixtures and sample/byte comparisons. Full-disc
test media and local comparison records are not distributed with this source.
Third-party source retains its original licenses and attribution under
third_party; build scripts and patches are included for rebuilding it.
